WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:05.790
TURNER: Good evening. 

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:11.259
My name's Dennis Turner from the Religious Studies department and the Divinity School. 

00:00:11.259 --> 00:00:13.349
Dean Attridge, who can't--

00:00:13.349 --> 00:00:15.569
and he apologizes for it--

00:00:15.569 --> 00:00:16.949
can't be here,

00:00:16.949 --> 00:00:17.670
asked me

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:19.710
to do the introduction.

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:24.749
And when he did, I was caused to reflect on the difference in style between the way Brits do it

00:00:24.749 --> 00:00:28.219
and the way it's done here. 

00:00:28.219 --> 00:00:29.789
Terry, you'll remember this.

00:00:29.789 --> 00:00:33.950
It works like this in a British university. I would say,

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:39.630
"There is absolutely no need to introduce so famous a person as Terry Eagleton," and I'd sit down,

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:43.000
because there's no need to do it. 

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:49.200
However, styles are more elaborate here, and rhetorical, and I thought it better-- I ought to say something.

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:52.140
And, Terry, you will remember 38 years ago,

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:56.570
you and I taught summer school in a place called Manhattanville College.

00:00:56.570 --> 00:00:59.680
And there are two things I remember from that. No, three.

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:03.329
One was: every day you got lost between the office and 

00:01:03.329 --> 00:01:05.280
your classroom.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:07.620
And we had to go and search for you. 

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:08.840
Secondly,

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:14.010
we discovered a most excellent television program called "Sesame Street."

00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:16.060
And--

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:23.060
in fact, I seem to remember you rearranging your classes so as to enable us to view it. 

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:29.090
Thirdly-- and this is MY memory-- I discovered the dialectic, from you. 

00:01:29.090 --> 00:01:33.640
And that-- I was caused to think about that by last week's lectures. 

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:34.860
Number one,

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:36.990
last Tuesday

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:38.799
was, to me, a perfectly

00:01:38.799 --> 00:01:42.890
marvelous re-narration, simply, of the Gospels. 

00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:44.270
Number two--

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:47.070
number one was with a view to getting

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:49.260
Ditchkins

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:52.950
to upgrade the standards of their critique. 

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:55.110
It's fairly clear--

00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:55.900
if

00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:57.260
it wasn't from 

00:01:57.260 --> 00:02:00.840
what Terry said--

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:03.150
there's a certain element of a straw man being

00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:04.560
knocked down. 

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:07.880
The second, on Thursday,

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:09.379
reminded us that

00:02:09.379 --> 00:02:12.290
actually the history of the uptake of those Gospels amounts

00:02:12.290 --> 00:02:16.349
to a fairly significant series of betrayals, and

00:02:16.349 --> 00:02:19.619
thirdly, 

00:02:19.619 --> 00:02:25.139
that there's something significant about this, that it's not a form of liberal even-handedness-- this has something to do with the 

00:02:25.139 --> 00:02:28.479
dialectic. Because what was really important about this was

00:02:28.479 --> 00:02:33.569
the better critique of institutional Christianity was actually to be found in the Gospels than in 

00:02:33.569 --> 00:02:35.809
Dawkins and Hitchens. 

00:02:35.809 --> 00:02:39.370
And that, I think, was something that you also
were saying about capitalism;

00:02:39.370 --> 00:02:44.499
it was something you were also saying about America
and American influence in foreign policy,

00:02:44.499 --> 00:02:47.500
and I think this is highly significant-- is that

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:47.980
actually

00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:53.420
the best form of critique is the auto-critique, which emerges from within the thing

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:55.899
which is being criticized.

00:02:55.899 --> 00:03:01.389
That, it seemed to me, said a great deal about,
as it were, the light-handed Marxism which is coming

00:03:01.389 --> 00:03:01.860
through,

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:04.309
particularly on Thursday's lectures. 

00:03:04.309 --> 00:03:05.749
What are we going to get today?

00:03:05.749 --> 00:03:08.579
I don't know. I hope Terry does.

00:03:08.579 --> 00:03:11.250
But it's supposed to be something in the territory

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:13.919
of faith and reason. Is that not right, Terry?

00:03:13.919 --> 00:03:20.919
Over to you.

00:03:26.439 --> 00:03:29.929
EAGLETON: And the fourth thing about Manhattanville College, Dennis, 

00:03:29.929 --> 00:03:33.569
was that-- wasn't it a massively beautiful thing--

00:03:33.569 --> 00:03:35.969
these, of course, were the days,

00:03:35.969 --> 00:03:37.869
the trendiest post-Vatican-II days, 

00:03:37.869 --> 00:03:38.650
one could imagine,

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:39.979
1970,

00:03:39.979 --> 00:03:42.409
with two hundred nuns--

00:03:42.409 --> 00:03:44.310
not, I assure you,

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:48.779
not penguin nuns, you know, but

00:03:48.779 --> 00:03:51.589
cigarette-smoking, high-heeled,

00:03:51.589 --> 00:03:54.109
be-lipsticked nuns. 

00:03:54.109 --> 00:03:54.379
Almost

00:03:54.379 --> 00:03:58.699
all of whom, I'm sure, now have at least four children and

00:03:58.699 --> 00:04:00.489
a very, very nice husband. 

00:04:00.489 --> 00:04:01.270
And

00:04:01.270 --> 00:04:02.969
there was a rather

00:04:02.969 --> 00:04:05.979
genial but crazed chaplain,

00:04:05.979 --> 00:04:07.089
if I remember,

00:04:07.089 --> 00:04:10.610
full of the effervescence of the spirit

00:04:10.610 --> 00:04:12.009
and of Vatican II who did 

00:04:12.009 --> 00:04:14.279
various bizarre masses,

00:04:14.279 --> 00:04:20.099
one of which, I think, he was dressed 
entirely in newspaper

00:04:20.099 --> 00:04:25.210
to-- I think to communicate some point about
communication, or something. 

00:04:25.210 --> 00:04:29.779
But then the piece de resistance was 
the so-called "Mass of the Beautiful

00:04:29.779 --> 00:04:30.440
Thing,"

00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:31.939
where he asked us, at the end of

00:04:31.939 --> 00:04:34.139
the previous week's mass,

00:04:34.139 --> 00:04:36.530
to bring next week some beautiful thing,

00:04:36.530 --> 00:04:39.970
adding, of course, "Bring yourselves, above all,"

00:04:39.970 --> 00:04:41.839
in his pious, phony,

00:04:41.839 --> 00:04:44.529
earnest, bogus way.

00:04:44.529 --> 00:04:45.399
And

00:04:45.399 --> 00:04:48.080
Dennis and I were late for mass and we had 
to

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:53.030
find a beautiful thing in about twenty
seconds. We found two identical fir cones

00:04:53.030 --> 00:04:55.639
outside the door-- you know, 

00:04:55.639 --> 00:04:57.270
cones dropped from the fir tree--

00:04:57.270 --> 00:04:59.870
and at one point we had solemnly to exchange--

00:04:59.870 --> 00:05:02.110
oh, we were asked first of all to 

00:05:02.110 --> 00:05:04.999
contemplate our beautiful thing, and so

00:05:04.999 --> 00:05:07.370
Dennis and I stared long

00:05:07.370 --> 00:05:10.039
and blankly at our fir cones. Then 

00:05:10.039 --> 00:05:12.630
we were asked even to smell them,

00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:17.829
and-- but certainly, at one point, to become them. 

00:05:17.829 --> 00:05:21.560
We both tried to feel suitably small, crinkled, and

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:22.489
dry. 

00:05:22.489 --> 00:05:27.129
And then we were asked to "exchange our beautiful thing" with another. Whereupon

00:05:27.129 --> 00:05:32.679
Dennis and I solemnly exchanged our

00:05:32.679 --> 00:05:34.679
identical fir cones.

00:05:34.679 --> 00:05:38.460
We will write a book on this at some point before we go. 

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:43.930
Yes, I am indeed going to talk about, this time, about faith
and reason.

00:05:43.930 --> 00:05:46.589
Something that I think 

00:05:46.589 --> 00:05:49.929
theologians and theologically minded people

00:05:49.929 --> 00:05:51.330
share with political

00:05:51.330 --> 00:05:53.939
radicals, and also with

00:05:53.939 --> 00:05:59.539
people of a psychoanalytic bent, Freudians and the rest, which is--

00:05:59.539 --> 00:06:01.240
the paradox that

00:06:01.240 --> 00:06:03.199
without reason,

00:06:03.199 --> 00:06:06.099
we're sunk, we're finished.

00:06:06.099 --> 00:06:07.309
Yet

00:06:07.309 --> 00:06:09.409
reason is not, in the end, 

00:06:09.409 --> 00:06:10.850
what is most fundamental

00:06:10.850 --> 00:06:11.919
about us.

00:06:11.919 --> 00:06:13.409
And keeping, as it were,

00:06:13.409 --> 00:06:17.800
both sides, both ends of that paradox, keeping a close
hand on both ends, 

00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:19.169
is pretty difficult.

00:06:19.169 --> 00:06:21.289
Richard Dawkins, in "The God

00:06:21.289 --> 00:06:26.080
Delusion," claims at one point with grandiloquent folly that

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:30.289
religious faith dispenses with reason altogether,

00:06:30.289 --> 00:06:32.210
which wasn't even true

00:06:32.210 --> 00:06:33.470
of the thick-

00:06:33.470 --> 00:06:35.340
headed clerics 

00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:37.919
who knocked me around at grammar school,

00:06:37.919 --> 00:06:39.559
high school.

00:06:39.559 --> 00:06:45.599
Even they didn't claim that they dispensed-- that religion dispensed with reason altogether. 

00:06:45.599 --> 00:06:50.009
Without-- so the paradox is: without reason, we
perish,

00:06:50.009 --> 00:06:52.989
but reason doesn't go all the way down. 

00:06:52.989 --> 00:06:54.649
A paradox

00:06:54.649 --> 00:06:56.520
splendidly, magnificently explored,

00:06:56.520 --> 00:06:59.069
I think, in some of the novels of Thomas Mann,

00:06:59.069 --> 00:07:03.129
in The Magic Mountain and Doktor Faustus particularly. You can't let go of reason,

00:07:03.129 --> 00:07:04.099
but reason

00:07:04.099 --> 00:07:05.090
isn't--

00:07:05.090 --> 00:07:07.780
will itself dwindle into a kind of 

00:07:07.780 --> 00:07:11.319
impoverished and brittle rationalism--

00:07:11.319 --> 00:07:15.569
of a kind not very far, I think, from Dawkins, at least--

00:07:15.569 --> 00:07:19.969
if it isn't, it doesn't recognize that it isn't all there is, 

00:07:19.969 --> 00:07:21.979
if it doesn't pay its dues, 

00:07:21.979 --> 00:07:24.580
if it doesn't, so to speak, acknowledge its 

00:07:24.580 --> 00:07:25.680
loyalty 

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:30.169
to some origins beyond itself. Without
reason, then, we perish, but

00:07:30.169 --> 00:07:34.120
the paradox is, reason doesn't go all the way down-- even Dawkins

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:38.819
has been known to emerge from his laboratory from
time to time.

00:07:38.819 --> 00:07:42.610
And there are even those uncharitable commentators who have detected

00:07:42.610 --> 00:07:46.300
the mildest whiff of obsessive irrationalism 

00:07:46.300 --> 00:07:48.609
in his zealous campaign for

00:07:48.609 --> 00:07:50.990
secular rationality. 

00:07:50.990 --> 00:07:53.039
And another old paradox, of course, is 

00:07:53.039 --> 00:07:54.010
"Push reason 

00:07:54.010 --> 00:07:55.380
to a certain limit,

00:07:55.380 --> 00:07:56.829
and it capsizes

00:07:56.829 --> 00:07:58.479
into its opposite."

00:07:58.479 --> 00:07:59.550
It capsizes

00:07:59.550 --> 00:08:01.529
into irrationalism. 

00:08:01.529 --> 00:08:03.879
Dawkins's anti-religioius zeal

00:08:03.879 --> 00:08:05.729
makes the Grand Inquisitory 

00:08:05.729 --> 00:08:08.619
look like a soggy liberal.

00:08:08.619 --> 00:08:13.679
Indeed, Dawkins seems to nurture a positively Maoist kind of faith 

00:08:13.679 --> 00:08:17.180
in faith itself. In the hopelessly

00:08:17.180 --> 00:08:21.639
idealist conception, for example, he seems to share

00:08:21.639 --> 00:08:23.779
that religious ideology

00:08:23.779 --> 00:08:27.469
is what fundamentally drives radical
Islam,

00:08:27.469 --> 00:08:31.129
as opposed to, say, material conditions or

00:08:31.129 --> 00:08:34.020
some perceived political injustice, 

00:08:34.020 --> 00:08:36.830
which it seems to be what most radical Islamists say

00:08:36.830 --> 00:08:38.199
is their motivation. 

00:08:38.199 --> 00:08:42.750
Dawkins sweeps that grandly aside in
his obsession with religion,

00:08:42.750 --> 00:08:48.450
and talks- and sees radical Islam itself
as, as it were, wholly motivated by religious

00:08:48.450 --> 00:08:49.670
ideas. His belief

00:08:49.670 --> 00:08:51.480
in the power of religion 

00:08:51.480 --> 00:08:57.430
is every bit as robust as the Pope's. 

00:08:57.430 --> 00:09:02.550
To claim that without reason we're sunk, yet 

00:09:02.550 --> 00:09:06.080
in the end reason isn't where it's at, as it were--

00:09:06.080 --> 00:09:09.330
and yet not to cave into

00:09:09.330 --> 00:09:12.730
one of the many varieties of irrationalism

00:09:12.730 --> 00:09:16.910
is as hard for the political radical
to sustain, 

00:09:16.910 --> 00:09:18.270
that fine

00:09:18.270 --> 00:09:20.060
tension, as I think it is for the

00:09:20.060 --> 00:09:22.880
theologian, or, indeed, for the Freudian. To

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:24.150
respect the ego--

00:09:24.150 --> 00:09:27.690
Freud, of course, the great rationalist, the great Enlightenment-- 

00:09:27.690 --> 00:09:29.190
radical Enlightenment tradition--

00:09:29.190 --> 00:09:31.150
and somebody who recognized, of course, 

00:09:31.150 --> 00:09:33.180
is not the master of its 

00:09:33.180 --> 00:09:35.190
own house, who showed us 

00:09:35.190 --> 00:09:36.329
magnificently

00:09:36.329 --> 00:09:37.600
how and why

00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:39.960
that was so.

00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:45.040
It's only if reason can draw upon resources

00:09:45.040 --> 00:09:46.190
more

00:09:46.190 --> 00:09:50.060
tenacious and less fragile than itself 

00:09:50.060 --> 00:09:51.350
that it's capable

00:09:51.350 --> 00:09:53.010
of prevailing. It

00:09:53.010 --> 00:09:54.600
can't prevail by itself.

00:09:54.600 --> 00:09:58.850
That's not to say that somebody else would 
prevail. Rather, that for reason itself 

00:09:58.850 --> 00:10:00.190
to prevail,

00:10:00.190 --> 00:10:04.600
it must constantly tap energies, resources, capacities,

00:10:04.600 --> 00:10:08.260
which in the end run deeper than reason itself.

00:10:08.260 --> 00:10:09.420
And that, I think, 

00:10:09.420 --> 00:10:12.560
is a truth which liberal rationalism 

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:14.480
of a Ditchkens variety

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:17.260
rather disastrously

00:10:17.260 --> 00:10:18.270
overlooks.

00:10:18.270 --> 00:10:20.020
And that brings us then 

00:10:20.020 --> 00:10:24.940
to the question of faith and reason, which is not, as
indeed I've tried to intimate

00:10:24.940 --> 00:10:25.370
here--

00:10:25.370 --> 00:10:28.760
is not just a theological question. What I've said there

00:10:28.760 --> 00:10:30.340
obviously has a reach, has a purview 

00:10:30.340 --> 00:10:33.940
beyond theology itself. 

00:10:33.940 --> 00:10:37.629
But I'll talk a little bit now about faith and reason
in a theological

00:10:37.629 --> 00:10:39.270
sense. 

00:10:39.270 --> 00:10:44.370
There is probably, I suppose, no greater evidence of Ditchkens's 
theological illiteracy

00:10:44.370 --> 00:10:46.090
than the fact that he appears

00:10:46.090 --> 00:10:50.080
to subscribe to what I previously called
the Yeti

00:10:50.080 --> 00:10:51.730
view of faith. 

00:10:51.730 --> 00:10:54.360
I mean by this, of course, the view

00:10:54.360 --> 00:10:55.110
that God is 

00:10:55.110 --> 00:10:56.970
a sort of object

00:10:56.970 --> 00:10:58.640
which, like the Yeti,

00:10:58.640 --> 00:11:02.370
or the ley lines, or the Loch Ness monster,

00:11:02.370 --> 00:11:03.900
or

00:11:03.900 --> 00:11:05.420
the tooth fairy,

00:11:05.420 --> 00:11:08.030
or unidentified flying objects--

00:11:08.030 --> 00:11:10.050
the evidence so far in

00:11:10.050 --> 00:11:12.320
is radically ambiguous, 

00:11:12.320 --> 00:11:13.569
not to say

00:11:13.569 --> 00:11:15.020
deeply dubious.

00:11:15.020 --> 00:11:20.180
And because we can't thus demonstrate God's existence in a reasonably straightforward

00:11:20.180 --> 00:11:20.910
way

00:11:20.910 --> 00:11:25.700
that we can demonstrate the existence of
necrophilia or Michael Jackson--

00:11:25.700 --> 00:11:29.640
then

00:11:29.640 --> 00:11:32.380
we have to put up with something

00:11:32.380 --> 00:11:34.590
less than certainty,

00:11:34.590 --> 00:11:37.550
known as faith. Well, 

00:11:37.550 --> 00:11:40.420
that's the Yeti view of things, shared

00:11:40.420 --> 00:11:43.150
as it were, by most atheists, most 

00:11:43.150 --> 00:11:46.690
agnostics, and, I'm afraid, most Christians too. 

00:11:46.690 --> 00:11:50.610
I scarcely, of course, need to point out to this 

00:11:50.610 --> 00:11:51.560
distinguished and

00:11:51.560 --> 00:11:54.170
utterly privileged and special company

00:11:54.170 --> 00:11:56.330
(he said grovelingly)--

00:11:56.330 --> 00:12:00.250
I scarcely need to point out to you what a
travesty

00:12:00.250 --> 00:12:02.650
of Christian faith, at least, this is. 

00:12:02.650 --> 00:12:06.580
So that on the most elementary questions of
the theology

00:12:06.580 --> 00:12:10.170
on which he chooses to pronounce with such

00:12:10.170 --> 00:12:12.440
portentously self-regarding authority,

00:12:12.440 --> 00:12:16.590
Ditchkens is hopelessly at sea. Even, as it were, at square one,

00:12:16.590 --> 00:12:18.890
he is. 

00:12:18.890 --> 00:12:21.930
For one thing, of course, God differs from the--

00:12:21.930 --> 00:12:26.199
you know, from unidentified flying objects or the Yeti or the tooth fairy

00:12:26.199 --> 00:12:30.750
in not being even a possible object of cognition. 

00:12:30.750 --> 00:12:34.270
It's just that we don't-- it's not just that we don't happen to be able to see him. 

00:12:34.270 --> 00:12:36.579
It is, as it were, that our not seeing him

00:12:36.579 --> 00:12:38.880
is inherent to God

00:12:38.880 --> 00:12:41.040
himself, which is presumably not true

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:41.980
of the Yeti,

00:12:41.980 --> 00:12:45.149
if he exists,

00:12:45.149 --> 00:12:50.930
or the tooth fairy. I'm sorry for anybody here 
who believed in the tooth fairy--

00:12:50.930 --> 00:12:53.840
I hasten to add, of course it does exist;

00:12:53.840 --> 00:12:56.860
don't worry about that at all. 

00:12:56.860 --> 00:12:57.800
For another thing,

00:12:57.800 --> 00:13:00.759
faith of course is traditionally regarded 

00:13:00.759 --> 00:13:03.310
as a question of certainty. 

00:13:03.310 --> 00:13:06.600
Even more scandalously for the Ditchkenses of this world,

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:08.650
not

00:13:08.650 --> 00:13:11.970
as a question of probability, or speculation, or guesswork,

00:13:11.970 --> 00:13:15.349
but actually as a question of certainty, which is not to say 

00:13:15.349 --> 00:13:21.030
that it's not also traditionally regarded as being inferior to knowledge, which it is. But only fully 

00:13:21.030 --> 00:13:23.679
paid-up rationalists 

00:13:23.679 --> 00:13:25.610
think that nothing is certain 

00:13:25.610 --> 00:13:28.850
but knowledge.

00:13:28.850 --> 00:13:31.730
Faith, as the author of the epistle to the Hebrews writes,

00:13:31.730 --> 00:13:33.750
is the assurance of things

00:13:33.750 --> 00:13:36.290
hoped for, conviction of things

00:13:36.290 --> 00:13:37.790
unseen. 

00:13:37.790 --> 00:13:41.210
Whatever else may divide science and theology

00:13:41.210 --> 00:13:47.630
or religion, it's from a theological view
certainly not the question of certainty. 

00:13:47.630 --> 00:13:51.150
I don't think Ditchkens understands that in the least. 

00:13:51.150 --> 00:13:54.450
The certainty in question, to be sure, 

00:13:54.450 --> 00:13:55.690
is not the same kind of 

00:13:55.690 --> 00:14:00.930
certainty as that of a well-entrenched scientific observation

00:14:00.930 --> 00:14:03.570
like, "It's just turned red"	

00:14:03.570 --> 00:14:04.139
or

00:14:04.139 --> 00:14:09.530
"The mouse is clearly drunk and we are now calling off the experiment" or

00:14:09.530 --> 00:14:14.560
"The experimenter is clearly drunk, so the mouse isn't."

00:14:14.560 --> 00:14:17.189
It's not a statement of that kind of certainty,

00:14:17.189 --> 00:14:18.409
to be sure, 

00:14:18.409 --> 00:14:22.780
but then neither are statements like, "I love you," which, as 

00:14:22.780 --> 00:14:26.530
Jonathan Kuller has pointed out, is always a quotation,

00:14:26.530 --> 00:14:27.810
the most quoted 

00:14:27.810 --> 00:14:29.730
piece of language. "I love you." Of course,

00:14:29.730 --> 00:14:33.130
however burningly or sincerely it's said,

00:14:33.130 --> 00:14:38.040
staring into their eyes at the time, is, of course
the most dreariest of quotations. 

00:14:38.040 --> 00:14:39.450
Totally intertextual. 

00:14:39.450 --> 00:14:43.170
It's not-- it's 

00:14:43.170 --> 00:14:47.650
statements-- certain statements like "I love you" 

00:14:47.650 --> 00:14:48.629
or, you know,

00:14:48.629 --> 00:14:53.070
"Liberal democracy is a lot better than slavery"

00:14:53.070 --> 00:14:58.430
or "The overweening Emma Woodhouse finally gets her comeuppance."

00:14:58.430 --> 00:15:01.450
There are not statements of a scientific
turn,

00:15:01.450 --> 00:15:05.480
but they are pretty certain for most of us, even so. 

00:15:05.480 --> 00:15:06.990
This isn't to claim, 

00:15:06.990 --> 00:15:10.689
as Dawkins seems to imagine in the
case of religion,

00:15:10.689 --> 00:15:12.500
that what one's really saying there

00:15:12.500 --> 00:15:16.750
is that religious propositions don't need evidence at all. 

00:15:16.750 --> 00:15:19.570
Dawkins makes the disastrous error, I think, of thinking

00:15:19.570 --> 00:15:22.080
that to say that they're not scientific 

00:15:22.080 --> 00:15:24.589
is therefore to say that they're not-- that they

00:15:24.589 --> 00:15:26.970
have no concern with the evidential

00:15:26.970 --> 00:15:28.840
whatsoever.

00:15:28.840 --> 00:15:32.970
If Jesus's body is mingled with the dust of Palestine, 

00:15:32.970 --> 00:15:36.570
then Christian faith is in vain. 

00:15:36.570 --> 00:15:38.490
It's rather to suggest that though,

00:15:38.490 --> 00:15:39.199
for example,

00:15:39.199 --> 00:15:42.010
I must be prepared to explain

00:15:42.010 --> 00:15:43.500
at tedious length

00:15:43.500 --> 00:15:47.610
what it is about you that I find so lovable, 

00:15:47.610 --> 00:15:49.809
to give all kinds of reasons for that,

00:15:49.809 --> 00:15:51.900
to argue the case, 

00:15:51.900 --> 00:15:56.310
adducing bodies of evidence, demonstrating
this or that,

00:15:56.310 --> 00:15:59.830
arguing over the existence or non-existence of this

00:15:59.830 --> 00:16:02.950
particular quality in you.

00:16:02.950 --> 00:16:06.290
I'm also forced to recognize that someone else 

00:16:06.290 --> 00:16:09.120
might see all this as well in you, and not love you

00:16:09.120 --> 00:16:12.690
at all. 

00:16:12.690 --> 00:16:17.080
The evidence itself will not decide the issue. 

00:16:17.080 --> 00:16:23.270
At some point along the line, a particular
way of seeing the evidence-- one, perhaps, implicit

00:16:23.270 --> 00:16:24.940
from the outset--

00:16:24.940 --> 00:16:26.550
comes into play,

00:16:26.550 --> 00:16:30.490
one which involves a particular kind of personal
engagement with it,

00:16:30.490 --> 00:16:32.740
and none of that is reducible 

00:16:32.740 --> 00:16:38.250
to the evidence itself, in the sense of being in love, to be motivated by

00:16:38.250 --> 00:16:39.710
a bare account 

00:16:39.710 --> 00:16:42.000
of a situation. 

00:16:42.000 --> 00:16:45.120
One might note, by the way, the difference between
that

00:16:45.120 --> 00:16:46.019
image 

00:16:46.019 --> 00:16:48.290
of faith and

00:16:48.290 --> 00:16:51.669
the dubious notion that reason can take us
so far,

00:16:51.669 --> 00:16:55.920
after which some kind of existential leap in the dark 

00:16:55.920 --> 00:16:56.800
proves to be essential. 

00:16:56.800 --> 00:16:58.350
I don't think that's

00:16:58.350 --> 00:16:59.870
the right model.

00:16:59.870 --> 00:17:05.840
You can know all you need to know--
as, let's say, a Germanist about the sonnets

00:17:05.840 --> 00:17:07.230
to Orpheus--

00:17:07.230 --> 00:17:11.550
but this is no guarantee that they won't leave you cold. 

00:17:11.550 --> 00:17:14.710
The evidence itself, as it were, will not move you, 

00:17:14.710 --> 00:17:17.630
necessarily, to a certain kind of engagement. 

00:17:17.630 --> 00:17:18.980
with the object. 

00:17:18.980 --> 00:17:23.250
And none of that, surely, should strike a scientist, a scientist 

00:17:23.250 --> 00:17:24.280
like Dawkins,

00:17:24.280 --> 00:17:26.180
as unfamiliar. 

00:17:26.180 --> 00:17:28.520
Because I take it that scientists are,

00:17:28.520 --> 00:17:29.720
in an important sense, 

00:17:29.720 --> 00:17:32.020
not only believers,

00:17:32.020 --> 00:17:36.770
but actually, in some sense, aestheticians. 

00:17:36.770 --> 00:17:38.890
You know, that

00:17:38.890 --> 00:17:41.930
elegance in science will get you a very,

00:17:41.930 --> 00:17:43.110
very long way.

00:17:43.110 --> 00:17:45.360
The universe is a matter of style.

00:17:45.360 --> 00:17:52.360
The truth of the universe is a matter of style. All scientists are closet Oscar Wildeans. 

00:17:53.810 --> 00:17:57.350
And as Rowan Williams remarks, all 

00:17:57.350 --> 00:17:57.890
communication

00:17:57.890 --> 00:17:59.310
involves faith. 

00:17:59.310 --> 00:18:02.510
All communication involves faith of some kind,

00:18:02.510 --> 00:18:04.820
and since reason

00:18:04.820 --> 00:18:08.700
is itself, of course, a collaborative and
dialogical and

00:18:08.700 --> 00:18:10.780
communicative affair, 

00:18:10.780 --> 00:18:15.190
and I think that the positivists often go sadly wrong by not seeing that,

00:18:15.190 --> 00:18:17.950
by seeing it as a much more, as it were,

00:18:17.950 --> 00:18:19.879
monistic affair--

00:18:19.879 --> 00:18:21.740
since reason is all a matter,

00:18:21.740 --> 00:18:26.320
indissociably a matter, of dialogue and communication,

00:18:26.320 --> 00:18:28.159
of collaboration, then--

00:18:28.159 --> 00:18:30.679
then if all communication involves faith,

00:18:30.679 --> 00:18:31.990
this must

00:18:31.990 --> 00:18:32.970
apply to reason

00:18:32.970 --> 00:18:34.440
too. Reason 

00:18:34.440 --> 00:18:40.280
is a dialogue, and dialogues always involve a kind of faith. 

00:18:40.280 --> 00:18:42.039
Nor, as we know-- 

00:18:42.039 --> 00:18:44.580
another familiar mistake made by

00:18:44.580 --> 00:18:47.160
the Ditchkenses of this world--

00:18:47.160 --> 00:18:50.890
nor is faith in the first place a matter of subscribing

00:18:50.890 --> 00:18:54.260
to the proposition that a supreme being

00:18:54.260 --> 00:18:55.610
exists. 

00:18:55.610 --> 00:18:56.779
For one thing, 

00:18:56.779 --> 00:19:00.960
God doesn't exist as an entity in the
world--

00:19:00.960 --> 00:19:03.080
atheists and believers are 

00:19:03.080 --> 00:19:03.520
agreed

00:19:03.520 --> 00:19:04.640
on this.

00:19:04.640 --> 00:19:07.090
And for another thing, as I have argued before, 

00:19:07.090 --> 00:19:08.169
in these lectures, 

00:19:08.169 --> 00:19:10.990
faith is in the first place performative

00:19:10.990 --> 00:19:12.809
rather than propositional. 

00:19:12.809 --> 00:19:14.560
Which is not to argue 

00:19:14.560 --> 00:19:17.170
that it's not

00:19:17.170 --> 00:19:18.700
coachable in terms of 

00:19:18.700 --> 00:19:20.040
propositions or, as we  

00:19:20.040 --> 00:19:21.470
traditionally say, doctrines. 

00:19:21.470 --> 00:19:23.340
Just that it's not in the first place

00:19:23.340 --> 00:19:24.090
a matter

00:19:24.090 --> 00:19:29.930
of descriptions of reality.

00:19:29.930 --> 00:19:32.080
It resembles utterances like 

00:19:32.080 --> 00:19:34.580
"I have faith in you"

00:19:34.580 --> 00:19:36.930
more than it does a statement like

00:19:36.930 --> 00:19:40.650
"I believe in goblins."

00:19:40.650 --> 00:19:42.280
Abraham has faith 

00:19:42.280 --> 00:19:45.700
but it's surely doubtful that he could even have conceived

00:19:45.700 --> 00:19:48.410
of God not existing. He had faith

00:19:48.410 --> 00:19:50.890
even so.

00:19:50.890 --> 00:19:54.400
The militant left-wing atheist Alain Badiou,

00:19:54.400 --> 00:19:57.360
probably the greatest living French philosopher, author 

00:19:57.360 --> 00:20:01.180
of an absolutely splendid book on St. Paul, 

00:20:01.180 --> 00:20:03.040
understands this far better 

00:20:03.040 --> 00:20:04.090
than does 

00:20:04.090 --> 00:20:06.720
his Anglo-Saxon liberal

00:20:06.720 --> 00:20:09.559
rationalist counterparts. 

00:20:09.559 --> 00:20:11.890
Badiou understands totally--

00:20:11.890 --> 00:20:13.560
as a Marxist, or

00:20:13.560 --> 00:20:18.420
or ex-Marxist, ex-Maoist, atheist, militant atheist--

00:20:18.420 --> 00:20:21.780
that faith articulates a loving commitment

00:20:21.780 --> 00:20:24.610
before it counts as a description of the way 

00:20:24.610 --> 00:20:25.580
things are, 

00:20:25.580 --> 00:20:29.250
though that it involves a description of the way
things are

00:20:29.250 --> 00:20:30.280
is as clear as

00:20:30.280 --> 00:20:32.580
the fact that moral imperatives. 

00:20:32.580 --> 00:20:36.050
do so, must do so, as well.

00:20:36.050 --> 00:20:38.860
Propositions that can be true or false have their roots in

00:20:38.860 --> 00:20:41.960
kinds of experiences which can't be treated 

00:20:41.960 --> 00:20:43.860
quite that way, and would not 

00:20:43.860 --> 00:20:45.350
be authentic 

00:20:45.350 --> 00:20:49.290
if they didn't have their roots in--

00:20:49.290 --> 00:20:55.850
one thinks, perhaps, of the nature of propositions in literature, or in fiction.

00:20:55.850 --> 00:21:02.190
Fiction contains all sorts of propositions, doesn't it? You know, that-- you know, 

00:21:02.190 --> 00:21:04.890
Paris is in France, or whatever, or snow is white. 

00:21:04.890 --> 00:21:09.250
It would simply be that-- those propositions can be falsified or verified, 

00:21:09.250 --> 00:21:12.559
it's simply-- it's certainly a fact that partly what we mean by fiction

00:21:12.559 --> 00:21:16.730
is that those propositions are not there for their own sake, as

00:21:16.730 --> 00:21:17.730
descriptions of the world. 

00:21:17.730 --> 00:21:20.379
They're there, as it were, as supports to

00:21:20.379 --> 00:21:24.500
a kind of rhetorical or performative attitude to the world,

00:21:24.500 --> 00:21:25.520
a stance to the world,

00:21:25.520 --> 00:21:27.500
which is what actually matters. 

00:21:27.500 --> 00:21:30.789
That's why so many propositions in fiction can

00:21:30.789 --> 00:21:32.840
actually be obviously false, and it

00:21:32.840 --> 00:21:39.750
doesn't really make any difference.

00:21:39.750 --> 00:21:41.870
Now I've got my pages mixed up again. 

00:21:41.870 --> 00:21:45.380
Yes, there we go. 

00:21:45.380 --> 00:21:47.570
Yet, as I say, this is not to say, of course, 

00:21:47.570 --> 00:21:50.570
that the truth or falsehood of such propositions 

00:21:50.570 --> 00:21:52.390
pales into nothing

00:21:52.390 --> 00:21:57.030
besides the depth of the experience from which
they spring.

00:21:57.030 --> 00:21:58.930
A believer, after all, 

00:21:58.930 --> 00:22:01.030
is someone in love,

00:22:01.030 --> 00:22:04.210
observes Kierkegaard in The Sickness Unto Death-- 

00:22:04.210 --> 00:22:09.330
a statement that of course by no means applies only to religious believers. 

00:22:09.330 --> 00:22:15.400
In St. Augustine's view, one can't arrive at truth except through a kind of love. 

00:22:15.400 --> 00:22:16.860
For St. Anselm, 

00:22:16.860 --> 00:22:19.130
reason itself is rooted in God, 

00:22:19.130 --> 00:22:23.070
so that faith must be reason's foundation. 

00:22:23.070 --> 00:22:25.910
This is what he means by-- part of what he means,
I suppose,

00:22:25.910 --> 00:22:28.300
by his celebrated statement, "I believe

00:22:28.300 --> 00:22:30.180
in order to understand."

00:22:30.180 --> 00:22:34.340
A proposition which, in a different sense,
many kinds of believers--

00:22:34.340 --> 00:22:36.490
socialists or feminists--

00:22:36.490 --> 00:22:38.770
could also make. 

00:22:38.770 --> 00:22:39.470
All reasoning,

00:22:39.470 --> 00:22:40.310
as we know,

00:22:40.310 --> 00:22:43.029
is conducted within the ambit of some

00:22:43.029 --> 00:22:44.900
kind of faith, however

00:22:44.900 --> 00:22:46.460
inarticulate it may be. 

00:22:46.460 --> 00:22:49.179
For Thomas Aquinas, love

00:22:49.179 --> 00:22:52.430
precedes truth and is the precondition of it. 

00:22:52.430 --> 00:22:55.630
We seek truth (for Thomas) because

00:22:55.630 --> 00:22:57.530
our material bodies

00:22:57.530 --> 00:22:58.980
manifest a built-in,

00:22:58.980 --> 00:23:01.710
ineradicable desire for it.

00:23:01.710 --> 00:23:02.789
A desire 

00:23:02.789 --> 00:23:05.739
which, for Aquinas, is the inscription within
us

00:23:05.739 --> 00:23:08.220
of divine love. 

00:23:08.220 --> 00:23:09.019
Without love, 

00:23:09.019 --> 00:23:11.950
no truth. 

00:23:11.950 --> 00:23:13.660
Faith, for Christian tradition, is

00:23:13.660 --> 00:23:16.090
actually what makes truth possible,

00:23:16.090 --> 00:23:19.399
which is true in an everyday sense as well.

00:23:19.399 --> 00:23:21.510
I think that faith is what makes truth possible--

00:23:21.510 --> 00:23:23.980
there's an oblique relation between that

00:23:23.980 --> 00:23:26.060
and Lenin's statement that

00:23:26.060 --> 00:23:30.390
theory would never be complete, that you would
never arrive at a complete

00:23:30.390 --> 00:23:31.580
formulation of
	
0:23:31.580,0:23:34.080
theory, Lenin once remarked, 

00:23:34.080 --> 00:23:34.580
without

00:23:34.580 --> 00:23:37.690
the development of a mass political movement--

00:23:37.690 --> 00:23:38.690
that is to say,

00:23:38.690 --> 00:23:42.740
only on the basis of a sort of practical kind of commitment, 
engagement

00:23:42.740 --> 00:23:45.200
with reality, politically speaking,

00:23:45.200 --> 00:23:46.350
can theory itself--

00:23:46.350 --> 00:23:49.450
a correct attempt to describe reality--

00:23:49.450 --> 00:23:51.800
come to fruition.

00:23:51.800 --> 00:23:56.710
It's because, perhaps, we've come to model
knowledge on things

00:23:56.710 --> 00:23:58.070
rather than on

00:23:58.070 --> 00:23:58.890
persons,

00:23:58.890 --> 00:24:01.070
that we fail to see

00:24:01.070 --> 00:24:03.620
this implication

00:24:03.620 --> 00:24:04.520
of knowledge

00:24:04.520 --> 00:24:06.940
and faith. That, for example,

00:24:06.940 --> 00:24:08.340
only by

00:24:08.340 --> 00:24:11.110
having faith and trust in

00:24:11.110 --> 00:24:12.090
somebody 

00:24:12.090 --> 00:24:14.520
can we really come to disclose ourself, ourselves,

00:24:14.520 --> 00:24:16.180
fully to them,

00:24:16.180 --> 00:24:17.930
and therefore be known. 

00:24:17.930 --> 00:24:19.070
That's one way, perhaps,

00:24:19.070 --> 00:24:20.220
among the manifold 

00:24:20.220 --> 00:24:21.390
number of ways 

00:24:21.390 --> 00:24:23.540
in which truth and faith go together.

00:24:23.540 --> 00:24:27.630
We have to have a certain faith and trust in
somebody else to be able to manifest ourselves

00:24:27.630 --> 00:24:28.660
to him or her

00:24:28.660 --> 00:24:31.930
in a full kind of way. 

00:24:31.930 --> 00:24:36.570
This is one of several senses, presumably, in which knowledge and virtue

00:24:36.570 --> 00:24:38.780
go together, because that

00:24:38.780 --> 00:24:42.940
self-manifestation involves a certain kind
of risk, it involves a certain 

00:24:42.940 --> 00:24:45.040
courage to do that. As the 

00:24:45.040 --> 00:24:46.200
Duke

00:24:46.200 --> 00:24:48.220
rebukes the slanderous Lucio

00:24:48.220 --> 00:24:50.600
in Shakespeare's Measure for Measure,

00:24:50.600 --> 00:24:52.450
"Love talks with better knowledge,

00:24:52.450 --> 00:24:55.860
and knowledge with dearer love."

00:24:55.860 --> 00:24:56.810
In the end, 

00:24:56.810 --> 00:25:00.490
only love, of which faith (ask Kierkegaard sees)

00:25:00.490 --> 00:25:04.200
is one form, the-- somebody, a believer, 

00:25:04.200 --> 00:25:06.429
is someone in love, as he says--

00:25:06.429 --> 00:25:11.610
only-- in the end, only love can 
achieve the well-nigh impossible goal

00:25:11.610 --> 00:25:15.049
of seeing the situation as it actually
is.

00:25:15.049 --> 00:25:15.720
Shorn 

00:25:15.720 --> 00:25:17.790
of both the deceptive 

00:25:17.790 --> 00:25:19.150
enchantments 

00:25:19.150 --> 00:25:20.450
of romance and the

00:25:20.450 --> 00:25:24.040
dishevelled fantasies of eros,

00:25:24.040 --> 00:25:25.640
love 

00:25:25.640 --> 00:25:26.840
endures to other, inferior 

00:25:26.840 --> 00:25:28.310
senses.

00:25:28.310 --> 00:25:32.530
Realism, of course, demands all kinds of virtues--

00:25:32.530 --> 00:25:36.809
I mean realism in the sense of simply trying to get to see the situation 

00:25:36.809 --> 00:25:38.760
as it actually is--

00:25:38.760 --> 00:25:43.280
demands all sorts of virtues, it's not some sort of clinical

00:25:43.280 --> 00:25:44.160
matter. 

00:25:44.160 --> 00:25:46.690
It demands an openness to being wrong,

00:25:46.690 --> 00:25:48.279
a certain humility,

00:25:48.279 --> 00:25:52.000
a certain selflessness, a certain

00:25:52.000 --> 00:25:53.750
preparedness for collaboration,

00:25:53.750 --> 00:25:56.090
a certain generosity of spirit,

00:25:56.090 --> 00:25:58.590
a lot of hard labor,

00:25:58.590 --> 00:26:00.100
conscientious judgment, and so on. 

00:26:00.100 --> 00:26:04.480
And for Aquinas all virtues, or such virtues, 

00:26:04.480 --> 00:26:06.660
have their roots in love. 

00:26:06.660 --> 00:26:09.269
So love is not irrelevant, and a very

00:26:09.269 --> 00:26:14.269
practical issue, as it were, of simply trying
to see the situation as it is

00:26:14.269 --> 00:26:16.210
which is of course the basis

00:26:16.210 --> 00:26:17.090
of all moral

00:26:17.090 --> 00:26:20.470
and political achievement. 

00:26:20.470 --> 00:26:22.220
The basis of all moral and political achievement is the 

00:26:22.220 --> 00:26:24.060
humble and essential matter 

00:26:24.060 --> 00:26:27.820
of trying to see things as they are. 

00:26:27.820 --> 00:26:30.230
A very, very basic and 

00:26:30.230 --> 00:26:31.840
low level enterprise

00:26:31.840 --> 00:26:36.460
which we have reason to believe may well be impossible. Love

00:26:36.460 --> 00:26:38.270
is the ultimate form 

00:26:38.270 --> 00:26:42.860
of a cold-eyed, soberly disenchanted realism,

00:26:42.860 --> 00:26:45.500
which is why it is the twin of truth. 

00:26:45.500 --> 00:26:46.660
The two--

00:26:46.660 --> 00:26:48.780
love and truth-- also have in common 

00:26:48.780 --> 00:26:54.180
the fact that they are both usually unpleasant. 

00:26:54.180 --> 00:26:57.070
I mean, radicals are people who hold that truth

00:26:57.070 --> 00:27:01.950
is usually a lot less palatable than they want us to believe--

00:27:01.950 --> 00:27:03.419
a necessary dash of 

00:27:03.419 --> 00:27:05.320
paranoia comes in there. 

00:27:05.320 --> 00:27:07.340
Nothing wrong with a bit of paranoia between

00:27:07.340 --> 00:27:09.620
friends, you know? 

00:27:09.620 --> 00:27:16.620
Just as people who don't believe in conspiracies 
are fantasists to the core, are these fantasists who don't believe

00:27:16.800 --> 00:27:19.919
in conspiracies. Strange. 

00:27:19.919 --> 00:27:26.110
And we've seen already, in our discussion of the New Testament, that there's nothing lovely about love. 

00:27:26.110 --> 00:27:32.910
We've seen just where love is likely to land you. 

00:27:32.910 --> 00:27:36.100
Pascal comments that the saints, he says, maintain that

00:27:36.100 --> 00:27:37.670
we must love things

00:27:37.670 --> 00:27:39.380
before we know them. Presumably

00:27:39.380 --> 00:27:43.690
because only through a passionate engagement with them 

00:27:43.690 --> 00:27:45.570
can we come to know them

00:27:45.570 --> 00:27:47.790
at all truly. 

00:27:47.790 --> 00:27:49.830
Dispassionateness is the death of knowledge, 

00:27:49.830 --> 00:27:53.100
in that sense of the word-- not in every sense of the word-- in that sense of the word,

00:27:53.100 --> 00:27:56.220
whatever the liberal may think. 

00:27:56.220 --> 00:27:59.820
I suppose there are some nowadays who

00:27:59.820 --> 00:28:04.070
would regard faith in socialism as even more eccentric

00:28:04.070 --> 00:28:06.460
than a belief in the Assumption of the Blessed Virgin

00:28:06.460 --> 00:28:09.790
into heaven. 

00:28:09.790 --> 00:28:12.420
Why, then

00:28:12.420 --> 00:28:14.800
do some of us still cling

00:28:14.800 --> 00:28:16.340
to this political faith,

00:28:16.340 --> 00:28:20.200
in the teeth of what many people would regard as recent 

00:28:20.200 --> 00:28:22.490
and solid evidence?

00:28:22.490 --> 00:28:25.080
Not only, I think, as I said before, 

00:28:25.080 --> 00:28:28.950
that socialism seems to be such an extraordinarily good idea, 

00:28:28.950 --> 00:28:31.890
that it's proved exceedingly hard to discredit, 

00:28:31.890 --> 00:28:37.980
and this despite its own very best efforts to do so. It's

00:28:37.980 --> 00:28:42.600
also, I think, not only that. It's because one can't
accept that

00:28:42.600 --> 00:28:46.450
for example, the world that we see groaning in agony

00:28:46.450 --> 00:28:47.780
around us, 

00:28:47.780 --> 00:28:49.760
is all there is 

00:28:49.760 --> 00:28:51.940
or all that could be.

00:28:51.940 --> 00:28:56.330
Even though we accept that, empirically speaking,
this might indeed be the case, this is perfectly

00:28:56.330 --> 00:28:57.769
possible. 

00:28:57.769 --> 00:29:02.519
It's also because one gazes with wondering bemusement

00:29:02.519 --> 00:29:03.840
upon those hard-headed 

00:29:03.840 --> 00:29:06.900
types for whom all this,

00:29:06.900 --> 00:29:08.520
all we see around us,

00:29:08.520 --> 00:29:11.410
perhaps given a reformist tweak or two,

00:29:11.410 --> 00:29:12.370
is as good

00:29:12.370 --> 00:29:13.810
as it gets. 

00:29:13.810 --> 00:29:17.000
One finds that utterly bewildering. Because 

00:29:17.000 --> 00:29:20.280
to back down from this vision would be to betray 

00:29:20.280 --> 00:29:23.860
what one feels are the most precious powers and capacities

00:29:23.860 --> 00:29:25.780
of human beings. 

00:29:25.780 --> 00:29:30.160
Because however hard one tries, one simply can't shake off the

00:29:30.160 --> 00:29:32.760
perhaps primitive or infantile 

00:29:32.760 --> 00:29:36.830
conviction that this is not how it is supposed to be.

00:29:36.830 --> 00:29:38.410
However much we're aware 

00:29:38.410 --> 00:29:43.900
that this seeing the world in the light of Judgment Day, as Walter Benjamin might put it, 

00:29:43.900 --> 00:29:46.350
is folly to the wise,

00:29:46.350 --> 00:29:50.700
and a stumbling block to stockbrokers-- 

00:29:50.700 --> 00:29:53.380
because there's something in this vision which
calls

00:29:53.380 --> 00:29:55.500
to the very depths of our being,

00:29:55.500 --> 00:29:57.460
and evokes a passionate

00:29:57.460 --> 00:29:59.240
assent there-- 

00:29:59.240 --> 00:30:02.490
because not to feel this would be not to be

00:30:02.490 --> 00:30:05.650
oneself-- would be not to be oneself--

00:30:05.650 --> 00:30:07.490
because one is too much in love 

00:30:07.490 --> 00:30:09.530
with this vision of humankind

00:30:09.530 --> 00:30:10.930
to back down,

00:30:10.930 --> 00:30:12.110
walk away,

00:30:12.110 --> 00:30:13.660
settle for half, or take

00:30:13.660 --> 00:30:14.579
no

00:30:14.579 --> 00:30:17.350
for an answer. 

00:30:17.350 --> 00:30:19.529
All of which, of course, as we can see from 

00:30:19.529 --> 00:30:21.070
Death of a Salesman,

00:30:21.070 --> 00:30:23.560
from Willy Loman, who is unable to back down,

00:30:23.560 --> 00:30:26.920
say no, take no for an answer, settle for half, or walk away,

00:30:26.920 --> 00:30:30.480
will lead directly to a certain kind of tragedy, to a kind of

00:30:30.480 --> 00:30:33.270
tragic impasse or death. Now,

00:30:33.270 --> 00:30:37.650
none of that seems to me to run counter
to reason--

00:30:37.650 --> 00:30:39.300
as it would, say, 

00:30:39.300 --> 00:30:42.630
if the world was already a nuclear wasteland 

00:30:42.630 --> 00:30:45.470
from which nothing could be salvaged-- then, indeed,

00:30:45.470 --> 00:30:46.650
those kinds of

00:30:46.650 --> 00:30:48.750
commitment could be said to be unreasonable.

00:30:48.750 --> 00:30:52.980
It's just that it's a different kind of thing
from a scientific observation

00:30:52.980 --> 00:30:54.609
or an everyday piece of cognition, as

00:30:54.609 --> 00:30:56.080
indeed, 

00:30:56.080 --> 00:30:58.450
Ditchkens's belief in the value

00:30:58.450 --> 00:31:00.110
of individual freedom

00:31:00.110 --> 00:31:01.590
is different 

00:31:01.590 --> 00:31:04.040
from a scientific observation. Ditchkens

00:31:04.040 --> 00:31:05.460
can't ground

00:31:05.460 --> 00:31:06.620
his beliefs

00:31:06.620 --> 00:31:09.549
in freedom, in freedom of inquiry, 

00:31:09.549 --> 00:31:14.399
and the need for rational debate-- he can't
ground those scientifically,

00:31:14.399 --> 00:31:16.970
and there's absolutely no reason, in my view,

00:31:16.970 --> 00:31:20.470
why he should be required to do so.

00:31:20.470 --> 00:31:21.380
These, then, 

00:31:21.380 --> 00:31:24.460
are perhaps some of the ways in which reason

00:31:24.460 --> 00:31:27.600
doesn't go all the way down.

00:31:27.600 --> 00:31:33.020
It's also the case, isn't it, that we need commitment to reason itself-- one which is not,

00:31:33.020 --> 00:31:34.660
perhaps, 

00:31:34.660 --> 00:31:36.580
reducible to reason. 

00:31:36.580 --> 00:31:38.950
We need to be committed to reason. 

00:31:38.950 --> 00:31:41.510
We can always ask ourselves, for example,

00:31:41.510 --> 00:31:43.290
why

00:31:43.290 --> 00:31:44.240
discovering the truth

00:31:44.240 --> 00:31:46.510
should be thought so desirable?

00:31:46.510 --> 00:31:48.220
Some people have thought it wasn't. 

00:31:48.220 --> 00:31:49.620
Nietzsche didn't think 

00:31:49.620 --> 00:31:52.179
it was very desirable, necessarily. 

00:31:52.179 --> 00:31:53.940
Nor, some of the time, 

00:31:53.940 --> 00:31:55.820
perhaps, did Henrik Ibsen

00:31:55.820 --> 00:31:57.660
or Joseph Conrad. 

00:31:57.660 --> 00:31:59.019
If we want rancor, malice, 

00:31:59.019 --> 00:32:03.860
resentment, anxiety, 

00:32:03.860 --> 00:32:05.530
or urged dominion, Nietzsche might inquire

00:32:05.530 --> 00:32:09.340
lurks behind this obdurate will to truth. 

00:32:09.340 --> 00:32:10.880
If we're to defend reason

00:32:10.880 --> 00:32:14.880
we must be inspired by more than reason to do so. 

00:32:14.880 --> 00:32:17.489
There are legitimate disputes, surely,

00:32:17.489 --> 00:32:21.270
over the nature and status of rationality itself,

00:32:21.270 --> 00:32:23.200
which are far from evolving

00:32:23.200 --> 00:32:25.180
any kind of irrationalism.

00:32:25.180 --> 00:32:28.610
To what extent, for example, reason encompasses

00:32:28.610 --> 00:32:29.570
the aesthetic, 

00:32:29.570 --> 00:32:30.760
imaginative,

00:32:30.760 --> 00:32:31.640
sensuous, 

00:32:31.640 --> 00:32:32.890
intuitive,

00:32:32.890 --> 00:32:34.630
and effectve.

00:32:34.630 --> 00:32:38.930
In what sense it might be, as I say, a dialogical affair?

00:32:38.930 --> 00:32:42.010
What counts as rational foundation?

00:32:42.010 --> 00:32:46.180
Whether reason inherently implicates the values of freedom, 

00:32:46.180 --> 00:32:47.100
autonomy,

00:32:47.100 --> 00:32:48.860
and self-determination?

00:32:48.860 --> 00:32:50.419
Whether it's substantive

00:32:50.419 --> 00:32:51.850
or procedural,

00:32:51.850 --> 00:32:52.850
apodictic 

00:32:52.850 --> 00:32:55.110
or evidential,

00:32:55.110 --> 00:32:55.850
instrumental

00:32:55.850 --> 00:32:57.120
or autotelic?

00:32:57.120 --> 00:32:58.320
To what extent

00:32:58.320 --> 00:33:03.150
it represents in its totalizing and apparently all-explanatory nature

00:33:03.150 --> 00:33:04.880
a recycled version 

00:33:04.880 --> 00:33:06.640
of the very mythologies that

00:33:06.640 --> 00:33:08.130
it seeks to oust,

00:33:08.130 --> 00:33:11.440
as Adorno and Horkheimer would argue?

00:33:11.440 --> 00:33:12.999
Whether, as I said before,

00:33:12.999 --> 00:33:16.840
it's to be modeled primarily on our knowledge of things 

00:33:16.840 --> 00:33:18.120
or our knowledge of persons?

00:33:18.120 --> 00:33:19.809
What relations the 

00:33:19.809 --> 00:33:21.370
rational ego 

00:33:21.370 --> 00:33:24.890
maintains with the superego, and the primary processes?

00:33:24.890 --> 00:33:29.520
What we make of the fact that even before
we've started to reason, 

00:33:29.520 --> 00:33:34.310
the world is in principle intelligible to us
in the first place?

00:33:34.310 --> 00:33:36.049
What if it wasn't?

00:33:36.049 --> 00:33:37.930
Could we imagine that?

00:33:37.930 --> 00:33:41.740
Whether it's true that we reason as we do

00:33:41.740 --> 00:33:45.100
primarily because of what we do?

00:33:45.100 --> 00:33:49.110
Whether reason is to be associated with common
sense and moderation,

00:33:49.110 --> 00:33:50.040
as it is

00:33:50.040 --> 00:33:51.679
with the liberal rationalists,

00:33:51.679 --> 00:33:52.559
or with

00:33:52.559 --> 00:33:53.580
revolution, 

00:33:53.580 --> 00:33:55.990
as it was by John Stuart Mill

00:33:55.990 --> 00:33:57.490
and the Jacobins?

00:33:57.490 --> 00:34:00.929
Whether reason only takes flight at dusk,

00:34:00.929 --> 00:34:03.980
whether it's to be contrasted with our animality,

00:34:03.980 --> 00:34:06.860
or seen as an integral part of it?

00:34:06.860 --> 00:34:09.540
And so on. Many questions we could raise

00:34:09.540 --> 00:34:11.359
about the status of nature, 

00:34:11.359 --> 00:34:14.489
of rationality, its relation to other things. 

00:34:14.489 --> 00:34:17.429
For Aquinas, to revert to him once again,

00:34:17.429 --> 00:34:18.899
and to quote

00:34:18.899 --> 00:34:19.909
Dennis Turner, 

00:34:19.909 --> 00:34:23.819
rationality is the form of our animality. 

00:34:23.819 --> 00:34:26.119
Bodiliness is the stuff

00:34:26.119 --> 00:34:27.819
of our intellectual being. 

00:34:27.819 --> 00:34:28.379
Theology

00:34:28.379 --> 00:34:30.290
in that sense is a species

00:34:30.290 --> 00:34:31.819
of materialism. 

00:34:31.819 --> 00:34:33.899
We reason as we do

00:34:33.899 --> 00:34:36.400
because of the kind of material creatures

00:34:36.400 --> 00:34:37.629
that we are.

00:34:37.629 --> 00:34:38.709
We're reasonable 

00:34:38.709 --> 00:34:40.419
because we're animals,

00:34:40.419 --> 00:34:41.620
not despite

00:34:41.620 --> 00:34:42.619
being so. 

00:34:42.619 --> 00:34:44.319
If an angel could speak, 

00:34:44.319 --> 00:34:48.179
we would not be able to understand what he said. 

00:34:48.179 --> 00:34:54.599
It's hard to feel that such considerations
lie at the forefront of the mind of Richard Dawkins,

00:34:54.599 --> 00:34:56.069
whose callow

00:34:56.069 --> 00:34:57.729
rationalistic complacency 

00:34:57.729 --> 00:35:00.390
is of just the sort that Jonathan Swift

00:35:00.390 --> 00:35:04.339
so magnificently savaged. 

00:35:04.339 --> 00:35:07.239
Swift, a rationalist, as has been said, who didn't believe in reason.

00:35:07.239 --> 00:35:13.409
It's equally hard to think that those questions
have ben much brooded over by Christopher Hitchens.

00:35:13.409 --> 00:35:16.679
In Robert Bolt's play, A Man For All Seasons, 

00:35:16.679 --> 00:35:21.609
Thomas More advances a very Catholic defense of reason. 

00:35:21.609 --> 00:35:22.849
Man, he says, 

00:35:22.849 --> 00:35:25.190
has been created by God to serve him

00:35:25.190 --> 00:35:27.880
in the wit and tangle and cunning

00:35:27.880 --> 00:35:29.410
of his mind. 

00:35:29.410 --> 00:35:32.529
Not simply in simplicity like the flowers, for example,

00:35:32.529 --> 00:35:34.979
but in the very cunning and deviousness

00:35:34.979 --> 00:35:37.489
of his intellect. That itself is a kind of

00:35:37.489 --> 00:35:39.099
service of God. 

00:35:39.099 --> 00:35:43.479
When a new version of the Oath of Allegiance
to the King is published,

00:35:43.479 --> 00:35:46.329
More eagerly asks his daughter Susanna 

00:35:46.329 --> 00:35:49.190
what the wording is. And his daughter Susanna replies in rather

00:35:49.190 --> 00:35:51.469
Protestant style,

00:35:51.469 --> 00:35:55.269
"Oh, Daddy, what do the words matter?"

00:35:55.269 --> 00:35:58.430
To which More replies, in his good old materialistic,

00:35:58.430 --> 00:36:00.039
Papistical way, 

00:36:00.039 --> 00:36:05.429
"An oath is made of words. I may be able to take it." Yet

00:36:05.429 --> 00:36:06.529
it's the same More,

00:36:06.529 --> 00:36:08.929
a stickler for the material, 

00:36:08.929 --> 00:36:10.850
the same More who, pressed by 

00:36:10.850 --> 00:36:12.400
the exasperated 

00:36:12.400 --> 00:36:13.180
Susanna 

00:36:13.180 --> 00:36:16.160
on why he won't declare his loyalty to the King, 

00:36:16.160 --> 00:36:20.889
berated by Susanna because-- she says,
"Daddy, it just isn't reasonable"--

00:36:20.889 --> 00:36:24.100
replies, "Well, in the end, it isn't a matter of reason. 

00:36:24.100 --> 00:36:28.989
In the end, it's a matter of love." 

00:36:28.989 --> 00:36:31.680
All reasoning is carried on within some

00:36:31.680 --> 00:36:32.279
kind of faith,

00:36:32.279 --> 00:36:35.930
including the faith in reason itself.

00:36:35.930 --> 00:36:37.659
For the philosopher Fichte,

00:36:37.659 --> 00:36:40.759
faith, though not necessarily of the religious variety,

00:36:40.759 --> 00:36:44.009
is prior to and foundational of 

00:36:44.009 --> 00:36:44.959
all knowledge. 

00:36:44.959 --> 00:36:48.079
Knowledge moves within the assumptions, as we know,

00:36:48.079 --> 00:36:52.209
which are embedded in our practical bound-up-ness with the world,

00:36:52.209 --> 00:36:54.999
and which can never be precisely

00:36:54.999 --> 00:36:55.919
formalized

00:36:55.919 --> 00:36:58.049
or thematized. 

00:36:58.049 --> 00:37:00.160
AS Wittgenstein remarks in On Certainty, 

00:37:00.160 --> 00:37:01.659
it is what we do

00:37:01.659 --> 00:37:03.330
that lies at the bottom of

00:37:03.330 --> 00:37:05.459
our language games. 

00:37:05.459 --> 00:37:07.310
Know-how

00:37:07.310 --> 00:37:08.900
precedes knowledge,

00:37:08.900 --> 00:37:11.339
and never ceases to underpin it. 

00:37:11.339 --> 00:37:13.999
This is not to concur 

00:37:13.999 --> 00:37:14.780
with the

00:37:14.780 --> 00:37:18.949
postmodern pragmatists and relativists,

00:37:18.949 --> 00:37:19.890
that reason

00:37:19.890 --> 00:37:24.249
is therefore too much on the inside of our way of life

00:37:24.249 --> 00:37:28.039
ever to operate as an effective critique of it. I mean
that

00:37:28.039 --> 00:37:28.829
argument might

00:37:28.829 --> 00:37:30.139
be thought to follow

00:37:30.139 --> 00:37:33.719
from the idea that reason,

00:37:33.719 --> 00:37:35.939
the roots of reason run down to what we do

00:37:35.939 --> 00:37:37.640
run down to our ways

00:37:37.640 --> 00:37:39.579
of doing things, our ways

00:37:39.579 --> 00:37:41.669
of carving up the world. 

00:37:41.669 --> 00:37:43.039
All right, 

00:37:43.039 --> 00:37:45.919
the postmodern pragmatist might say

00:37:45.919 --> 00:37:51.569
that means that reason is absolutely bound up with our culture, is, as it were, on the inside of it, 

00:37:51.569 --> 00:37:54.249
and therefore the idea that reason could ever

00:37:54.249 --> 00:37:55.009
put enough

00:37:55.009 --> 00:37:56.929
daylight between itself and

00:37:56.929 --> 00:37:58.010
what we do to operate

00:37:58.010 --> 00:37:59.970
as a critique 

00:37:59.970 --> 00:38:00.890
of what we do

00:38:00.890 --> 00:38:02.339
is an entire

00:38:02.339 --> 00:38:04.729
fantasy. Reason would there have to be

00:38:04.729 --> 00:38:06.889
standing at some Olympian or

00:38:06.889 --> 00:38:08.829
Archimedean point

00:38:08.829 --> 00:38:13.609
which simply doesn't exist in the first place. 
Well, I don't think that's right, I think that's

00:38:13.609 --> 00:38:16.609
a mistake.

00:38:16.609 --> 00:38:19.039
This is the idea that the criteria

00:38:19.039 --> 00:38:21.379
which determine

00:38:21.379 --> 00:38:23.969
what is to count as reasonable 

00:38:23.969 --> 00:38:26.089
must themselves be derived 

00:38:26.089 --> 00:38:27.480
from the very form of life

00:38:27.480 --> 00:38:31.019
that they seek to put under critical scrutiny. The

00:38:31.019 --> 00:38:32.420
very criteria which, to 

00:38:32.420 --> 00:38:34.049
count as what is reasonable, 

00:38:34.049 --> 00:38:38.069
and therefore which seem to offer a kind of
overview, 

00:38:38.069 --> 00:38:40.579
a monitoring of our way of life,

00:38:40.579 --> 00:38:43.190
actually themselves, according to this view, are drawn from our way of life

00:38:43.190 --> 00:38:45.130
and have no 

00:38:45.130 --> 00:38:46.290
basis in any other

00:38:46.290 --> 00:38:46.749
place. 

00:38:46.749 --> 00:38:49.809
I think what that forgets, among other things,

00:38:49.809 --> 00:38:51.230
is that 

00:38:51.230 --> 00:38:53.080
it's part of the way

00:38:53.080 --> 00:38:56.120
in which the human animal is actually bound up

00:38:56.120 --> 00:38:57.469
with the world,

00:38:57.469 --> 00:39:01.469
that it's able to distance itself from it. 

00:39:01.469 --> 00:39:02.869
Not least by labor and language. 

00:39:02.869 --> 00:39:06.109
It's not the kind of

00:39:06.109 --> 00:39:07.409
bogus

00:39:07.409 --> 00:39:11.150
transcendence that we can leap out of our skins 

00:39:11.150 --> 00:39:12.200
and leap out of our world. 

00:39:12.200 --> 00:39:15.430
It's actually part of the way we're in the world

00:39:15.430 --> 00:39:17.029
as laboring, linguistic

00:39:17.029 --> 00:39:17.900
creatures,

00:39:17.900 --> 00:39:19.990
communicative creatures, relational creatures--

00:39:19.990 --> 00:39:22.769
that we're able, to some degree, to get kind of fixed

00:39:22.769 --> 00:39:24.369
upon it in the way 

00:39:24.369 --> 00:39:25.610
that it seems

00:39:25.610 --> 00:39:27.739
squirrels and goldfish 

00:39:27.739 --> 00:39:29.349
can't. I don't wish to 

00:39:29.349 --> 00:39:34.049
be odiously patronizing or condescending
to squirrels, or goldfish, you know, 

00:39:34.049 --> 00:39:35.680
they're my 

00:39:35.680 --> 00:39:38.249
best friends, 

00:39:38.249 --> 00:39:42.749
I just want to say-- they just don't look as though they're engaging in a ferocious fundamental critique,

00:39:42.749 --> 00:39:44.369
you know, of the world that they're

00:39:44.369 --> 00:39:47.489
actually a part of. And of course, because they're not, they are

00:39:47.489 --> 00:39:49.149
marvelously safe. 

00:39:49.149 --> 00:39:51.430
Because they're so integrated with their environments,

00:39:51.430 --> 00:39:55.539
because they're not cursed and blessed with that awful 

00:39:55.539 --> 00:39:57.709
double-edged sword called language,

00:39:57.709 --> 00:39:59.229
or developed consciousness, 

00:39:59.229 --> 00:40:00.589
they're not capable,

00:40:00.589 --> 00:40:05.400
you know, of bringing themselves, of overreaching
themselves, and bringing themselves to

00:40:05.400 --> 00:40:06.329
nothing,

00:40:06.329 --> 00:40:10.479
as a linguistic animal alone is. 

00:40:10.479 --> 00:40:15.440
So the doxa, or implicit certainties, or taken-for-granted truths,	

00:40:15.440 --> 00:40:19.339
which constitute the matrix of all our more formal 

00:40:19.339 --> 00:40:19.959
reasoning, 

00:40:19.959 --> 00:40:23.249
are as obvious in the case of science, I suppose,

00:40:23.249 --> 00:40:25.579
as much as anywhere else. 

00:40:25.579 --> 00:40:27.810
This much at least, one might say,

00:40:27.810 --> 00:40:29.430
the postmodern skeptics have

00:40:29.430 --> 00:40:30.869
going for them

00:40:30.869 --> 00:40:32.649
where it comes to science,

00:40:32.649 --> 00:40:34.759
though one should bear in mind

00:40:34.759 --> 00:40:39.240
that humanists have always been prejudiced against scientists.

00:40:39.240 --> 00:40:41.100
It's just that over the years

00:40:41.100 --> 00:40:43.219
they've shifted their grounds

00:40:43.219 --> 00:40:46.739
for this. I mean, scientists used to be-- you know, in my day, 

00:40:46.739 --> 00:40:49.589
scientists used to be regarded as unspeakable 

00:40:49.589 --> 00:40:54.760
oiks with dandruff on their collar and leather patches on their sports jackets' 

00:40:54.760 --> 00:40:55.429
elbows

00:40:55.429 --> 00:40:57.269
who thought that Rimbaud

00:40:57.269 --> 00:41:00.130
was a cinematic strong man

00:41:00.130 --> 00:41:03.569
rather than a French poet. 

00:41:03.569 --> 00:41:06.229
They've now become, for a later 

00:41:06.229 --> 00:41:08.550
epoch, the authoritarian custodians of 

00:41:08.550 --> 00:41:11.439
absolute truth. Yes. The

00:41:11.439 --> 00:41:12.619
peddlers now

00:41:12.619 --> 00:41:15.259
of the postmodern objection to science,

00:41:15.259 --> 00:41:19.029
of an obnoxious ideology known as objectivity, 

00:41:19.029 --> 00:41:22.720
a notion which simply tars up the ideological

00:41:22.720 --> 00:41:23.040
prejudices

00:41:23.040 --> 00:41:25.109
with an acceptably disinterested 

00:41:25.109 --> 00:41:26.000
guise. 

00:41:26.000 --> 00:41:27.860
Interestingly, one thing that's happened in our time

00:41:27.860 --> 00:41:29.079
is that the

00:41:29.079 --> 00:41:32.170
burden of the critique of science, the resistance to science, 

00:41:32.170 --> 00:41:34.509
has shifted from a tradition humanism

00:41:34.509 --> 00:41:36.359
to a kind of culturalism. 

00:41:36.359 --> 00:41:37.770
Culturalism is now

00:41:37.770 --> 00:41:41.170
one of a long series, a rather dishonorable series,

00:41:41.170 --> 00:41:43.199
of critics of science.

00:41:43.199 --> 00:41:43.810
Culturalism, I mean

00:41:43.810 --> 00:41:46.109
that intellectually violent attempt

00:41:46.109 --> 00:41:49.199
to repress or eradicate nature

00:41:49.199 --> 00:41:50.339
altogether

00:41:50.339 --> 00:41:52.839
in the name of culture. 

00:41:52.839 --> 00:41:56.179
One doesn't need to subscribe, of course, to the absurd caricature

00:41:56.179 --> 00:41:59.059
to note that science, like every other human 

00:41:59.059 --> 00:41:59.979
practice

00:41:59.979 --> 00:42:02.269
is indeed shot through with prejudice

00:42:02.269 --> 00:42:05.070
and partisanship and unthematizable 

00:42:05.070 --> 00:42:06.219
assumptions,

00:42:06.219 --> 00:42:12.619
unconscious biases, beliefs too close to the eyeball to be objectified, and so on. 

00:42:12.619 --> 00:42:14.229
If the Virgin Mary 

00:42:14.229 --> 00:42:18.819
were to put in an appearance at this very moment over the skies in
New Haven, 

00:42:18.819 --> 00:42:25.819
clutching the baby jesus with one hand and 
nonchalantly distributing 100 dollar notes with the other, 

00:42:25.819 --> 00:42:31.260
it would be more than the reputation of anybody
laboring away on the Yale laboratories was worth

00:42:31.260 --> 00:42:35.739
even fractionally to put their heads out of the window. 

00:42:35.739 --> 00:42:37.229
There are still, in other words, 

00:42:37.229 --> 00:42:40.270
a good many telescopes up which
science

00:42:40.270 --> 00:42:42.759
is churlishly disinclined

00:42:42.759 --> 00:42:43.559
to peer. 

00:42:43.559 --> 00:42:47.150
There are those topics which in a Foucaultian phrase are in the truth, 

00:42:47.150 --> 00:42:49.429
in the truth

00:42:49.429 --> 00:42:51.200
at any given time,

00:42:51.200 --> 00:42:53.019
and those which happen not to be

00:42:53.019 --> 00:42:54.219
in the truth at a particular time. 

00:42:54.219 --> 00:42:56.169
Marxism

00:42:56.169 --> 00:42:58.130
used to be in the truth, quite recently;

00:42:58.130 --> 00:42:59.350
it isn't now.

00:42:59.350 --> 00:43:04.249
It's not so much that it's run out of facts; it's that
it's out of favor. 

00:43:04.249 --> 00:43:07.039
I happen to know, for example, as a fact

00:43:07.039 --> 00:43:08.230
that the moon

00:43:08.230 --> 00:43:11.299
deeply affects human behavior.

00:43:11.299 --> 00:43:14.799
Since I'm a mild kind of lunatic, 

00:43:14.799 --> 00:43:16.949
I'm always aware of when the moon

00:43:16.949 --> 00:43:18.009
is full,

00:43:18.009 --> 00:43:19.519
even without looking,

00:43:19.519 --> 00:43:22.609
if I don't go quite as far as baying or

00:43:22.609 --> 00:43:25.019
sprouting hair on my cheeks.

00:43:25.019 --> 00:43:30.259
But I doubt that any scientist who valued
their corporate grants would fall over themselves

00:43:30.259 --> 00:43:33.720
to investigate this remarkably well-researched,

00:43:33.720 --> 00:43:35.650
well-evidenced phenomenon. 

00:43:35.650 --> 00:43:36.720
It would be rather like a literary 	

00:43:36.720 --> 00:43:39.169
critic publishing a three-volume work on 

00:43:39.169 --> 00:43:44.249
Goosy Goosy Gander, yes.

00:43:44.249 --> 00:43:46.610
Though Dawkins's The God Delusion 

00:43:46.610 --> 00:43:48.309
is astonishingly tightly,

00:43:48.309 --> 00:43:50.329
astonishingly laconic about

00:43:50.329 --> 00:43:54.279
the cock-ups and

00:43:54.279 --> 00:43:55.849
catastrophes of science--

00:43:55.849 --> 00:44:00.519
Dawkins castigates the Inquisition, but not Hiroshima--

00:44:00.519 --> 00:44:03.749
not the world that science has actually helped to create.

00:44:03.749 --> 00:44:04.740
Most of us are

00:44:04.740 --> 00:44:09.520
surely aware that like almost any interesting human pursuit, from staging a play 

00:44:09.520 --> 00:44:11.240
to running the economy,

00:44:11.240 --> 00:44:14.159
science is a lot more dicey and precarious,

00:44:14.159 --> 00:44:16.599
anomalous, and serendipitous

00:44:16.599 --> 00:44:19.709
than its publicity agents would prefer us to think,

00:44:19.709 --> 00:44:23.859
and that many of its practitioners will go
to quite extraordinary lengths

00:44:23.859 --> 00:44:24.590
to preserve

00:44:24.590 --> 00:44:26.430
a tried and trusted 

00:44:26.430 --> 00:44:28.709
hypothesis. 

00:44:28.709 --> 00:44:30.759
None of that is in my view

00:44:30.759 --> 00:44:31.780
to discredit

00:44:31.780 --> 00:44:33.950
that loving,

00:44:33.950 --> 00:44:35.299
passionate, selfless, 

00:44:35.299 --> 00:44:37.559
faithful, exhausting,

00:44:37.559 --> 00:44:39.070
profoundly ethical labor

00:44:39.070 --> 00:44:42.419
known as trying to get it right.

00:44:42.419 --> 00:44:44.289
In political life, it's a labor

00:44:44.289 --> 00:44:47.719
which can make the difference between life and death. 

00:44:47.719 --> 00:44:51.330
It's also one which, for all its necessary
demythologizing,

00:44:51.330 --> 00:44:53.750
could be said to have religious roots.

00:44:53.750 --> 00:44:54.610
Charles Taylor

00:44:54.610 --> 00:44:58.769
has pointed out how the scientifically detached 
or disinterested

00:44:58.769 --> 00:45:00.369
subject of modernity 

00:45:00.369 --> 00:45:02.979
has its historical origins, or some of them,

00:45:02.979 --> 00:45:04.340
in premodern

00:45:04.340 --> 00:45:05.019
religious

00:45:05.019 --> 00:45:06.069
asceticism,

00:45:06.069 --> 00:45:08.129
with its contemptuous mundi, 

00:45:08.129 --> 00:45:10.909
in order to know the world 

00:45:10.909 --> 00:45:11.999
in strange irony, 

00:45:11.999 --> 00:45:13.319
you must at the same time reject it. 

00:45:13.319 --> 00:45:17.299
In a similar way, Heidegger regards the Cartesian notion of certainty 

00:45:17.299 --> 00:45:19.900
as a hangover from the certainty of

00:45:19.900 --> 00:45:22.329
religious faith. 

00:45:22.329 --> 00:45:24.219
Even so, yet

00:45:24.219 --> 00:45:28.270
the fact remains that there are those for the sake of whose flourishing

00:45:28.270 --> 00:45:29.980
and--

00:45:29.980 --> 00:45:33.249
those who for the sake of their flourishing and well-being

00:45:33.249 --> 00:45:34.400
need to know

00:45:34.400 --> 00:45:38.479
more or less how things stand with them. 

00:45:38.479 --> 00:45:39.889
Not everybody. Not everyone needs to know that. 

00:45:39.889 --> 00:45:41.400
But there are those people

00:45:41.400 --> 00:45:43.489
so placed in society

00:45:43.489 --> 00:45:49.190
that in order to flourish and to have some well-being, 
they need to know, rather insistently,

00:45:49.190 --> 00:45:51.289
on how things stand with them.

00:45:51.289 --> 00:45:53.950
And there are those rather more privileged souls, 

00:45:53.950 --> 00:45:57.519
some of them known as postmodernists, for whom this represents, simply,

00:45:57.519 --> 00:45:59.549
an ideological illusion. 

00:45:59.549 --> 00:46:01.330
Some people need knowledge in order to

00:46:01.330 --> 00:46:03.100
do important things with it

00:46:03.100 --> 00:46:06.049
and others don't. 

00:46:06.049 --> 00:46:09.910
So science is, to that extent, surely about faith as well, 

00:46:09.910 --> 00:46:12.059
which is not all it shares in common

00:46:12.059 --> 00:46:13.359
with theology. 

00:46:13.359 --> 00:46:17.239
Rather, as the church is largely said to have betrayed

00:46:17.239 --> 00:46:18.890
the historical mission,

00:46:18.890 --> 00:46:20.769
so, one might argue, 

00:46:20.769 --> 00:46:22.269
has a great deal of science. 

00:46:22.269 --> 00:46:24.099
I myself was twenty years

00:46:24.099 --> 00:46:26.459
a fellow of Wadham College, Oxford, 

00:46:26.459 --> 00:46:29.229
an institution which in the late seventeenth century

00:46:29.229 --> 00:46:31.349
was home to the Royal Society,

00:46:31.349 --> 00:46:33.520
one of whose founders, John Wilkins, 

00:46:33.520 --> 00:46:34.920
was the Warden of Wadham,

00:46:34.920 --> 00:46:36.360
and was the brother-in-law 

00:46:36.360 --> 00:46:38.059
of Oliver Cromwell. 

00:46:38.059 --> 00:46:39.969
Unlike most of the rest of Oxford, 

00:46:39.969 --> 00:46:43.190
the College was on the progressive side of the Civil War, 

00:46:43.190 --> 00:46:45.939
and suffered rather badly for it, 

00:46:45.939 --> 00:46:49.019
and Wadham's rather traditionally dissident

00:46:49.019 --> 00:46:49.699
politics

00:46:49.699 --> 00:46:54.779
lasted as long as into the twentieth century
in the form of a kind of Bloomsbury 

00:46:54.779 --> 00:46:57.040
liberalism, basically reflected by 

00:46:57.040 --> 00:46:57.930
its

00:46:57.930 --> 00:47:00.989
twentieth century Warden Sir Maurice Bowra 

00:47:00.989 --> 00:47:02.289
who was rash enough to 

00:47:02.289 --> 00:47:04.439
take me on as a young Fellow, 

00:47:04.439 --> 00:47:07.639
largely, I think, because he fancied me, in fact, but--

00:47:07.639 --> 00:47:08.699
something that I feel rather 

00:47:08.699 --> 00:47:13.529
disreputable about. My career, you know, is based upon the

00:47:13.529 --> 00:47:19.459
attractions of a rather elderly Bloomsbury person. 

00:47:19.459 --> 00:47:24.080
I really don't like to think about that too much. I'd like to think it was 

00:47:24.080 --> 00:47:28.339
part of my merit that I was elected an Oxford Fellow, you know, not just for 

00:47:28.339 --> 00:47:30.859
disreputable reasons of this kind. 

00:47:30.859 --> 00:47:33.349
All of this had its roots in

00:47:33.349 --> 00:47:35.459
this revolutionary

00:47:35.459 --> 00:47:39.199
scientific lineage, which of course is 

00:47:39.199 --> 00:47:44.229
part of a history which those today who are sometimes rather glibly critical of science seem to forget, rather conveniently, 

00:47:44.229 --> 00:47:48.780
a lineage which prized fidelity to one's reasoning, experience, 

00:47:48.780 --> 00:47:50.770
and independent good sense

00:47:50.770 --> 00:47:52.610
over loyalty to prelate

00:47:52.610 --> 00:47:54.179
and monarch.

00:47:54.179 --> 00:47:59.989
The fact that, like religion, much of scientific practice
has betrayed its own revolutionary impulses, 

00:47:59.989 --> 00:48:03.349
is becoming the obedient tool of corporate interests,

00:48:03.349 --> 00:48:05.219
shouldn't induce us, I think, to forget

00:48:05.219 --> 00:48:08.909
this emancipatory history. 

00:48:08.909 --> 00:48:11.449
I had more things to say, 

00:48:11.449 --> 00:48:13.339
but not all that much, actually. 

00:48:13.339 --> 00:48:16.679
I had-- I was going to go on to talk about

00:48:16.679 --> 00:48:18.559
how far faith is 

00:48:18.559 --> 00:48:20.179
something that we choose, 

00:48:20.179 --> 00:48:24.079
how far we find ourselves, as it were--

00:48:24.079 --> 00:48:26.920
what we find ourselves believing something,

00:48:26.920 --> 00:48:28.259
what that means,

00:48:28.259 --> 00:48:30.269
or whether that's deterministic or not. 

00:48:30.269 --> 00:48:33.219
But I've said enough, so I will 

00:48:33.219 --> 00:48:33.629
open it to discussion if I may. Thank you. 

